# Docker's build context does NOT consult .gitignore, and is uploaded to the Docker
# daemon in full (minus what this file excludes) BEFORE any Dockerfile instruction
# runs — independent of which files a Dockerfile later chooses to COPY. Since the
# package-layout restructure, Dockerfile carries an ALLOWLIST of three explicit COPY
# lines (requirements.txt, pyproject.toml, src/) and no `COPY . .`, so this file no
# longer decides what reaches the final image — the allowlist does. This file's one
# remaining job: personal data must not leave the machine via the build context at
# all, and that context should stay small. This file and .gitignore still have to be
# maintained together for the personal-data rules specifically (enforced by
# tests/test_ignore_files.py), even though the consequence of a gap changed shape.
#
# This was written after an early image (built with a since-removed `COPY . .`) was
# found to contain 9.4 MB of real candidates' résumés and a live 104 enterprise
# session cookie jar. The exclusion had been stated as a hard constraint in three
# documents and enforced against git alone.

# Real candidates' résumés, names, e-mail addresses, phone numbers, and raw traffic
# recordings from a live enterprise session.
research/captures/

# Live session cookies and the local SQLite database. The cookies are a working 104
# enterprise session; the database is bind-mounted at runtime anyway, so baking a
# copy in only creates a stale one that also carries candidate ids.
data/

# Not needed at runtime, and .git carries the full history of everything above.
.git/
.gitignore
.spec/
tests/
docs/
*.md

# ⚠ Root-scoped, deliberately NOT widened to `**/*.md`: Docker's ignore matching is
# filepath.Match-based, where a single `*` does not cross a `/`. Widening this rule
# would silently exclude src/mcp104/assets/104-categories/README.md and
# src/mcp104/assets/certification/README.md — both cited from module docstrings and
# both required package data (`[tool.setuptools.package-data]` in pyproject.toml) —
# even though `src/` is itself allowlisted by the Dockerfile's COPY line; a build
# context that never contained them in the first place cannot ship them regardless of
# what the COPY line asks for.
#
# This is the SAME `filepath.Match` mechanism the "Local noise" rules below get `**/`
# prefixes for, pointed the opposite direction: there, root-scoping was the bug
# (excluding nothing at the nested location that actually needs it); here, root-
# scoping is the fix (widening would exclude files that must stay). Two neighbouring
# sections deliberately handling the same mechanism oppositely — read both before
# changing either.

# Local noise
#
# `**/` matters here, not cosmetic: Docker's ignore matching anchors a single `*` at
# the context root, and only `**/` crosses a `/` to reach nested occurrences — the
# same filepath.Match semantics the `*.md` rule above relies on, applied in the
# OPPOSITE direction. `__pycache__/`, `*.pyc` and `*.egg-info/` all genuinely occur at
# depth in this repo (e.g. src/mcp104/tools/__pycache__/, and — created directly by
# the `pip install -e ".[dev]"` this project's own dev setup requires —
# src/mcp104.egg-info/, one level inside src/, not at the root). A root-scoped rule
# for any of these would silently exclude nothing at the actual location while still
# passing a presence-only check that a rule merely exists.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.venv/
venv/
**/*.egg-info/

# `build/` and `dist/` are, BY CONTRAST, genuinely root-only for this project: both
# are setuptools' own build-tool output, always written relative to wherever
# `pyproject.toml` is invoked from — which for every build/install path this project
# actually uses (the Dockerfile's `RUN pip install ... .`, and any local `python -m
# build`) is the repository root, never a subdirectory. Root-scoped is therefore
# correct here, not merely convenient — `**/` would cost nothing but would also be
# asserting a depth risk that does not exist for these two.
build/
dist/
